TimelockController::state(id) — branching-tree (BTT) exemplar
See the Testing section of the Solidity standards (branching-tree technique). Each leaf maps to a
named test in TimelockControllerTest.t.sol; a "given" is a state-setup modifier, a "when" is a
parameter branch, an "it should" is the asserted outcome. Source: TimelockControllerLib.state().

state(id)
├── given the operation timestamp is zero (never scheduled)
│   └── it should return Unset            → test_OperationStateUnsetBeforeSchedule
└── given the operation timestamp is non-zero
    ├── given the timestamp equals the DONE sentinel
    │   └── it should return Done          → test_ExecuteMarksOperationDone
    └── given the timestamp is not the DONE sentinel
        ├── given the timestamp is in the future (delay not elapsed)
        │   └── it should return Waiting   → test_OperationStateWaitingAfterSchedule
        └── given the timestamp is not in the future (delay elapsed)
            └── it should return Ready      → test_OperationStateReadyAfterDelay
